UI-EP-001 — BTA User Interface Standards

Document ID: UI-EP-001
Title: Bible Therapy Assistant™ User Interface Standards
Status: Draft – Phase 2A
Owner: BTA Platform Architecture
Applies To: All BTA user interface presentation components

1. Purpose

This document establishes the User Interface (UI) standards governing the visual presentation of Bible Therapy Assistant™ (BTA).

The purpose of this standard is to ensure the BTA interface presents a consistent, accessible, professional, and Scripture-centered experience across all pages and devices while supporting the constitutional principles established for the platform.

This document governs presentation only.

2. Scope

UI standards include, but are not limited to:

Navigation layout
Page layout
Header and footer presentation
Typography
Color standards
Visual hierarchy
Icons
Buttons
Links
Responsive presentation
Accessibility presentation
Component appearance
Mobile presentation
3. Relationship Between UI and UX

Although User Interface (UI) and User Experience (UX) are closely related, they serve different purposes within the BTA governance model.

User Interface (UI)

User Interface governs how the application appears to the reader.

Examples include:

Navigation placement
Header design
Footer design
Typography
Color selection
Visual hierarchy
Component styling
Responsive layout
Accessibility presentation

UI answers the question:

"What does the reader see?"

User Experience (UX)

User Experience governs how the reader interacts with the application to accomplish a task.

Examples include:

Scripture search workflow
Connect workflow
Contact workflow
Donation workflow
Progressive Disclosure
Reader journey
Confirmation messaging
Error recovery
Workspace transitions

UX answers the question:

"How does the reader accomplish their objective?"

4. Governance Principle

User Interface standards shall govern presentation.

User Experience standards shall govern interaction workflows.

The two disciplines are complementary but shall remain independently governed to allow presentation changes without altering workflows, and workflow improvements without requiring presentation redesign.

5. Design Objective

The BTA interface shall communicate:

Trust
Clarity
Calmness
Accessibility
Professionalism

while keeping God's Word as the central focus of the reader experience.

Presentation elements shall support Scripture engagement rather than compete for the reader's attention.

I especially like Section 3 because it gives future developers and designers a clear constitutional boundary:

UI = appearance.
UX = interaction.

That simple distinction will help prevent future governance documents from overlapping and gives you a natural place for a future UX-EP-001 when you begin documenting workflows such as Connect, Contact, Donate, and Progressive Disclosure.
